    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. It's a bit hard to visualize the air movement part though. Is there a way to describe the actual quality of the sound? Like, why does the /s/ sound so much more \"sharp\" or \"hissing\" compared to the /z/ which sounds more like a \"hum\" or a \"buzz\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"sharpness\" or \"hiss\" of the /s/ sound comes from the tighter constriction between the tongue and the upper teeth (the alveolar ridge). This tight, narrow passage restricts the air, leading to a high-speed flow of air that causes the high-pitched, sibilant sound, resulting in that characteristic hiss.\n\nOn the other hand, the \"hum\" or \"buzz\" of the /z/ sound is due to the vibration of the vocal cords, which adds a periodic low-frequency component to the sound. This vibration, along with softer airflow and a less constricted contact between the tongue and teeth, contributes to a calmer, humming quality compared to the hissing sound of /s/. The contrast is essentially between a high-frequency hissing caused by airflow restriction, versus a low or mid-frequency buzz generated by vocal cord vibration.",
      "source": "target",
